WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.359 --> 00:00:04.029
Since the announcement that Jared Leto was
going to be appearing as Morbius in an upcoming

00:00:04.029 --> 00:00:07.520
Sony film, movie fans across the world have
been asking the same question: who the heck

00:00:07.560 --> 00:00:08.740
is Morbius?

00:00:09.120 --> 00:00:12.980
The short answer is that he’s a vampire
whose curse comes science rather than magic,

00:00:13.160 --> 00:00:17.040
who operates sometimes a villain, sometimes
as a hero, and often a bit of both.

00:00:17.660 --> 00:00:22.880
Beyond that simple concept, though, is a complicated
character, a tormented soul seeking salvation…

00:00:23.220 --> 00:00:23.720
and blood.

00:00:23.900 --> 00:00:26.540
Here's the untold truth of Morbius, the Living
Vampire.

00:00:27.160 --> 00:00:28.260
Blood-sucking fiend?

00:00:28.800 --> 00:00:33.210
Morbius tends to be a good guy by default
these days, but when he first showed up in

00:00:33.210 --> 00:00:35.620
1971's Amazing Spider-Man #101?

00:00:35.860 --> 00:00:40.300
He was tragic and he was tormented, but there
was no anti-hero nonsense going on.

00:00:40.540 --> 00:00:41.720
Morbius was a killer.

00:00:41.920 --> 00:00:46.040
That issue was memorable for a few reasons;
most notably being that it was the first issue

00:00:46.040 --> 00:00:48.400
of the comic that wasn't written by Stan Lee.

00:00:48.690 --> 00:00:52.860
Roy Thomas took over writing duties, picking
up on the cliffhanger ending where Peter Parker

00:00:52.860 --> 00:00:54.000
had six arms.

00:00:54.160 --> 00:00:58.040
Spider-Man sought out the Nobel-prize winning
Dr. Michael Morbius in hopes that he could

00:00:58.040 --> 00:01:02.110
find a cure, but instead found a bloodsucking
vampire who looked like he raided The Bee

00:01:02.110 --> 00:01:02.940
Gees' wardrobe.

00:01:03.380 --> 00:01:07.470
Thomas considered Morbius to be a one-shot
enemy for Spider-Man, but he also knew there

00:01:07.470 --> 00:01:09.680
was more to him than just simple super-villainy.

00:01:09.910 --> 00:01:13.680
As he said in a 2009 interview, "Both sides
of him were always there.

00:01:13.680 --> 00:01:15.840
He was always supposed to be a fairly sympathetic
character."

00:01:16.700 --> 00:01:18.140
Morbius vs the Code

00:01:18.820 --> 00:01:23.300
The Comics Code was a self-imposed industry
standard that among other things banned all

00:01:23.300 --> 00:01:26.660
representations of the undead starting in
1954.

00:01:27.260 --> 00:01:31.720
Some fans believe that Morbius was referred
to as a "living" vampire as a sneaky way of

00:01:31.729 --> 00:01:35.549
getting around the Comics Code, and that his
subsequent popularity helped break it down

00:01:35.549 --> 00:01:36.120
for good.

00:01:36.400 --> 00:01:39.800
His "living" status was Marvel's loophole...
or so the legend told.

00:01:40.160 --> 00:01:44.380
The truth is that his creation was actually
a response to the Comics Code having already

00:01:44.380 --> 00:01:48.040
loosened its rules about vampires, zombies,
and other undead creatures.

00:01:48.200 --> 00:01:52.280
Since they were no longer restricted under
the code's old rules, Thomas and Lee considered

00:01:52.290 --> 00:01:57.049
using the most famous vampire of all and making
Dracula the villain in Amazing Spider-Man

00:01:57.049 --> 00:01:57.549
#101.

00:01:57.680 --> 00:02:01.980
Instead, Lee opted for a twist on the classic
vampire as a costumed super-villain although

00:02:02.060 --> 00:02:06.860
Marvel would launch Tomb of Dracula the following
year, giving the original blood-sucking count

00:02:06.860 --> 00:02:08.560
a series that ran for 70 issues.

00:02:08.800 --> 00:02:13.580
Ironically, while Morbius' creation didn't
create any friction with the Comics Code,

00:02:13.590 --> 00:02:17.261
another Marvel artist claimed to have locked
horns with the Code over his work on the character

00:02:17.261 --> 00:02:18.080
years later.

00:02:18.420 --> 00:02:23.170
Penciler Ron Wagner worked on the first fourteen
issues of Morbius: The Living Vampire, and

00:02:23.170 --> 00:02:28.110
in 1993, he told Wizard Magazine he got in
trouble with the Code for sexually explicit

00:02:28.110 --> 00:02:29.100
drawings in the comic.

00:02:30.020 --> 00:02:31.320
Operative word: living

00:02:31.860 --> 00:02:36.340
While Morbius' "living" status wasn’t meant
to sneak around the Comics Code, it was an

00:02:36.340 --> 00:02:39.840
important aspect of his character if for no
other reason than the fact that it allowed

00:02:39.840 --> 00:02:43.660
him to remain in the Marvel Universe far longer
than his undead contemporaries.

00:02:44.500 --> 00:02:49.739
In 1983's Doctor Strange #62, the Sorcerer
Supreme famously used his magic to boost a

00:02:49.740 --> 00:02:54.460
magic spell called the Montesi Formula, killing
Dracula along with every other vampire on

00:02:54.460 --> 00:02:54.960
Earth.

00:02:55.280 --> 00:02:58.980
Eventually, Dracula would make his return
coming back from the dead is just what that

00:02:58.980 --> 00:03:01.420
dude does but it would take a full decade.

00:03:01.660 --> 00:03:06.700
In the meantime one vampire remained completely
unaffected by any of it: our man Morbius.

00:03:07.040 --> 00:03:11.340
Because his condition had nothing to do with
being bitten by some seductive undead temptress,

00:03:11.340 --> 00:03:16.120
or stabbing a church, Doctor Strange's war
on Dracula had no impact on Morbius.

00:03:16.690 --> 00:03:19.820
Other than the fact that he probably had a
lot more food available considering all the

00:03:19.820 --> 00:03:20.900
competition was dust.

00:03:21.740 --> 00:03:23.500
To Infect, or not to infect

00:03:23.980 --> 00:03:28.380
Over the past few hundred years, people have
defined vampirism pretty thoroughly, especially

00:03:28.380 --> 00:03:31.750
after Bram Stoker gave us all the blueprint
in 1897.

00:03:32.360 --> 00:03:35.880
Morbius, on the other hand, is all over the
place, including the question of whether his

00:03:35.880 --> 00:03:37.820
bites can create other vampires.

00:03:38.400 --> 00:03:42.620
Even when he drinks someone’s blood, they
only become vampires, like, sometimes.

00:03:43.360 --> 00:03:47.380
The first character he ever transformed was
Jefferson Bolt in Marvel Team-Up #3.

00:03:47.840 --> 00:03:52.560
Unlike traditional vampire spawn, Bolt retained
his free will and intelligence, eventually

00:03:52.560 --> 00:03:53.580
turning on Morbius.

00:03:54.280 --> 00:03:58.600
More often than not, however, The Living Vampire’s
victims either die from the normal injuries

00:03:58.610 --> 00:04:02.970
you’d expect from having your neck snacked
on, or survive them with the usual mundane

00:04:02.970 --> 00:04:03.760
consequences.

00:04:03.960 --> 00:04:08.620
Whether or not his abilities allow him to
infect others has less to do with his condition,

00:04:08.620 --> 00:04:10.900
and way more to do with what’s convenient
for the story.

00:04:11.400 --> 00:04:12.940
A little help from my She-Hulk

00:04:13.300 --> 00:04:17.280
One of the most important stories in Morbius'
history, the tale that transformed him from

00:04:17.299 --> 00:04:21.970
a tragic villain into a dark hero, involved
a weirdly violent court scene in the pages

00:04:21.970 --> 00:04:23.020
of Savage She-Hulk.

00:04:23.430 --> 00:04:28.220
Temporarily cured of his pseudo-vampire condition,
Dr. Morbius was working on ways to help another

00:04:28.220 --> 00:04:32.639
character with an interesting blood-related
problem: lawyer Jennifer Walters, whose gamma

00:04:32.640 --> 00:04:36.420
powered blood transfusion from her cousin
Bruce Banner turned her into the She-Hulk.

00:04:36.760 --> 00:04:41.040
While helping her, Morbius was arrested, and
when Walters defended him during the trial,

00:04:41.040 --> 00:04:43.280
she used a bizarre method to protect her client.

00:04:43.629 --> 00:04:48.260
To prove Morbius was not in control of his
actions while he was a vampire, Walters brought

00:04:48.260 --> 00:04:52.540
a pair of rabbits into the court and fed one
of them the formula that had transformed him.

00:04:52.820 --> 00:04:56.980
Predictably, the rabbit immediately went full
Holy Grail and killed its furry mate.

00:04:59.580 --> 00:05:01.100
“...I warned you!”

00:05:01.240 --> 00:05:05.880
Walters succeeded in getting Morbius' charges
reduced, but the public wasn't happy.

00:05:05.889 --> 00:05:09.759
Right after the jury read its decision, her
father told her he was ashamed of her, which

00:05:09.760 --> 00:05:12.440
might be even more painful than what happened
to that poor rabbit.

00:05:12.900 --> 00:05:14.120
Story vs gory

00:05:14.780 --> 00:05:19.460
When Morbius got his first shot at a solo
title in 1992, it began with the creative

00:05:19.460 --> 00:05:21.540
team of Len Kaminski and Ron Wagner.

00:05:22.060 --> 00:05:26.140
In spite of the passion Kaminski felt for
the project, his time on the title was short-lived.

00:05:26.660 --> 00:05:27.340
The reason?

00:05:27.600 --> 00:05:29.800
Apparently he wrote too much story.

00:05:30.280 --> 00:05:35.560
Speaking to Back Issue magazine in 2009, Kaminski
described Morbius as one of his dream projects,

00:05:35.560 --> 00:05:40.229
and said that he’d plotted 50 issues of
the series when he started, including a crossover

00:05:40.229 --> 00:05:41.680
with Spider-Man and Venom.

00:05:42.020 --> 00:05:44.980
Unfortunately, he was fired from the book
after only eight issues.

00:05:45.380 --> 00:05:49.300
Kaminski recalled that that he wasn't sure
why editor Bobbie Chase fired him, but in

00:05:49.319 --> 00:05:54.629
a 1993 Wizard article, Ron Wagner said he
was tired of Kaminski's character-driven stories,

00:05:54.629 --> 00:05:57.340
that he wanted more action and more gore,
and that Chase agreed.

00:05:57.550 --> 00:06:01.480
When Wizard reached out to Kaminski for comment,
the writer said Wagner used to leave snide

00:06:01.490 --> 00:06:05.610
notes in the margins of Kaminski's scripts,
and that he tried to call Wagner to talk over

00:06:05.610 --> 00:06:07.400
changes, but never got a response.

00:06:07.760 --> 00:06:12.260
Once Chase fired Kaminski, colorist Gregory
Wright gave Wagner the gorier scripts he wanted...

00:06:12.400 --> 00:06:13.660
for a little while.

00:06:14.000 --> 00:06:16.440
Wagner himself was gone from the book by #14.

00:06:17.000 --> 00:06:17.880
Ultimate Morbius

00:06:18.420 --> 00:06:22.539
When Jared Leto brings Morbius to the big
screen, there’s a chance he won't be a "living"

00:06:22.540 --> 00:06:23.160
vampire at all.

00:06:23.360 --> 00:06:28.520
Ultimate Spider-Man #95 introduced an updated
version of Morbius who had a much bigger connection

00:06:28.520 --> 00:06:32.240
to the vampires of his world than the regular
Marvel Universe’s version.

00:06:32.740 --> 00:06:37.330
He was a more traditional, undead vampire
who told Spider-Man he was the son of Dracula,

00:06:37.330 --> 00:06:40.580
and spent his time hunting his brethren and
operating as a villain.

00:06:41.089 --> 00:06:45.819
While Marvel officially put the nail in the
Ultimate line’s coffin in 2015, its impact

00:06:45.820 --> 00:06:47.800
on the film adaptations can't be ignored.

00:06:48.080 --> 00:06:51.480
A lot of what we've seen in the MCU was first
seen in the Ultimate Comics.

00:06:52.060 --> 00:06:56.439
Nick Fury was first drawn as Samuel L. Jackson
in The Ultimates six years before the actor

00:06:56.439 --> 00:06:57.610
appeared as the S.H.I.E.L.D.

00:06:57.610 --> 00:06:58.460
Director in Iron Man.

00:06:58.780 --> 00:07:02.949
Black Widow and Hawkeye being a part of the
original Avengers line-up, Captain America's

00:07:02.949 --> 00:07:08.050
World War II era uniform, the invading alien
soldiers from the first Avengers film, and

00:07:08.050 --> 00:07:11.780
plenty of other visual elements seen in the
movies were lifted from the Ultimate line.

00:07:11.900 --> 00:07:16.280
There's no reason to believe the Morbius filmmakers
wouldn't learn from the MCU's example and

00:07:16.280 --> 00:07:18.900
draw on a much less complicated version of
the character.

00:07:19.240 --> 00:07:21.340
Morbius versus Blade… or not

00:07:21.640 --> 00:07:26.040
While he never actually made it to the screen
for a film adaptation, Leto’s casting isn’t

00:07:26.120 --> 00:07:28.550
the first time that Morbius has had a brush
with Hollywood.

00:07:28.840 --> 00:07:33.379
In fact, if screenwriter David Goyer's original
plan had been realized, the Living Vampire's

00:07:33.380 --> 00:07:37.320
first big-screen appearance would likely have
pre-dated the MCU by eight years.

00:07:37.680 --> 00:07:42.719
The DVD for 1998’s Blade, written by Goyer,
features a deleted scene in which a mysterious

00:07:42.719 --> 00:07:46.949
figure in a dark trench coat watches from
a nearby rooftop as Blade and Karen emerge

00:07:46.949 --> 00:07:49.420
triumphant from their conflict with La Magra.

00:07:49.809 --> 00:07:54.740
In 2016, David Goyer confirmed the figure
was intended to be Morbius and, had he gone

00:07:54.749 --> 00:07:58.439
on to direct Blade II, Morbius would have
been the villain for the sequel.

00:07:58.439 --> 00:08:02.460
Instead, Guillermo del Toro directed Blade
II and the story was altered to pit Blade

00:08:02.460 --> 00:08:03.860
against the Reapers instead.

00:08:04.200 --> 00:08:08.080
By the time Goyer directed Blade: Trinity,
the focus had shifted to Hannibal King and

00:08:08.080 --> 00:08:09.560
the spinoff-ready Nightstalkers.

00:08:10.000 --> 00:08:14.119
All three movies wound up featuring stone-cold
evil bad guys, which makes sense since we

00:08:14.119 --> 00:08:18.259
all want to see Blade chop their heads off,
but presenting a more tragic and sympathetic

00:08:18.259 --> 00:08:20.979
foe could’ve made for a very different,
very interesting movie.

